One is that it has been on the top 100 list for longer than any
other game.  Heck, pick any month where a historical list for
top 100 that can be found an add up the longevity of all of the
top 10 entries - I don't think you'll ever find a time when all of
the top 10 put together match the top 100 longevity of NetHack.
Yet NetHack has never gotten into the top 10.

Longevity is one type of popularity.  Compare the album that
has the longest longevity - I think it was Pink Floyd's Dark
Side of the Moon that was on the top 100 list for over 20
years yet never hit the top 10.  Compare Hank Aaron whose
record of consequetive games in baseball wasn't matched for
decades yet he wasn't the top hitter or whatever the whole
time.

I tend to go with a record of longevity like chess or go or The
Greatful Dead over a meteoric rise and fall like so many
musical one-hit-wonder groups or games that are popular this
year but not next year.

Classics get their status from longevity not from popularity.
In movies it's like the Academy Awards that sometimes stay
as classics, yet the highest grossing movie of the year is
rarely still known decades later.  (The exceptions in movies
get interesting, and to map that into games we now have
cell phone versions of Pac Man).


